      en: "Cousin Island is a 27-hectare nature reserve in the Indian Ocean, established in 1968 as a bird sanctuary. It is home to one of the largest populations of the Seychelles warbler (Acrocephalus sechellensis), a species once on the brink of extinction. Today, over 3,000 individuals inhabit the island. The island is also a critical nesting site for hawksbill and green turtles. Visitors can join guided tours to observe endemic birds, giant tortoises, and pristine beaches. The island is rat- and cat-free, ensuring a safe habitat for native species.",
